ADDART({id:"carnotaurus",t:"Carnotaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦖",w:"Carnotaurus",r:["trex","allosaurus","dino","fossils","paleontology","argentinosaurus"],
p:[["The meat-eating bull","Its name means meat-eating bull, after the horns above its eyes."],["Tiny arms","Its arms were even smaller for its size than those of T. rex."],["Skin impressions","Fossils preserved patches of its scaly skin."]],
l:"Carnotaurus was a large meat-eating dinosaur that lived in what is now Argentina about 72 to 69 million years ago, near the end of the age of dinosaurs. It grew to about 8 meters long and weighed around 1.5 tonnes. Its name means ‘meat-eating bull’, after the two thick horns that stuck out above its eyes, which it may have used for display or in shoving contests with rivals. Carnotaurus had a short, deep skull, long powerful legs and extremely small arms, with stubby forearms that could barely move, proportionally even tinier than those of Tyrannosaurus rex. Its long legs and tail muscles suggest it was one of the faster large predators of its time. It belonged to a group called abelisaurids, which were the top predators in the southern continents. Only one skeleton is known, found in 1984 in Patagonia, but it is remarkably complete and includes large patches of fossilized skin covered in small scales and rows of bigger bumps.",
s:[["Discovery","The skeleton was found in 1984 by an expedition led by the Argentine paleontologist José Bonaparte in Chubut Province, Patagonia. It was named in 1985. The bones were encased in a hard rock nodule, which helped preserve the skin impressions."],["Built for speed","A large muscle at the base of the tail, the caudofemoralis, pulled the thigh back with each stride. Studies suggest Carnotaurus had especially large tail muscles, so it may have been a fast runner, though not very good at turning sharply."],["Scaly skin","Unlike some smaller dinosaurs that had feathers, Carnotaurus seems to have been covered in small, non-overlapping scales with scattered larger, cone-shaped bumps along its sides. It is one of the best-known examples of skin in a large meat-eating dinosaur."],["Abelisaurids","Abelisaurids lived in South America, Africa, India and Madagascar. They had short snouts, rough skulls and very small arms. Relatives include Majungasaurus from Madagascar and Abelisaurus from Argentina."]],
k:[["Lived","About 72–69 million years ago"],["Where","Argentina (Patagonia)"],["Length","About 8 meters"],["Diet","Meat"],["Group","Abelisaurid theropod"],["Discovered","1984, by José Bonaparte’s team"]],
d:[["1984","Skeleton found in Chubut, Argentina"],["1985","Carnotaurus named"],["2016","Study describes its large tail muscles"]],
s2:[["Horns","No other large meat-eating dinosaur had horns like Carnotaurus. Some scientists think males may have butted heads or pushed each other with them."],["Movie star","Carnotaurus has appeared in films and video games, often shown chasing larger dinosaurs."]],
g:[["Theropod","A two-legged, mostly meat-eating dinosaur"],["Abelisaurid","A family of short-snouted southern theropods"],["Patagonia","The southern region of Argentina and Chile"],["Skin impression","A fossil imprint of skin"]],
f:["Carnotaurus had horns above its eyes.","Carnotaurus lived in South America.","Its arms were very small."],
fun:"Carnotaurus’s forearms were so short that its hands seemed to sprout almost directly from its elbows."});

ADDART({id:"microraptor",t:"Microraptor",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🪶",w:"Microraptor",r:["archaeopteryx","dinobirds","velociraptor","birds","fossils","paleontology"],
p:[["Four wings","It had long feathers on both its arms and legs."],["Crow-sized","It was about the size of a crow."],["Shiny black","Fossil pigments show its feathers were glossy black."]],
l:"Microraptor was a small feathered dinosaur that lived in the forests of what is now northeastern China about 120 million years ago. About 80 centimeters long including its tail and weighing around a kilogram, it was roughly the size of a crow. Its most remarkable feature was that it had long flight feathers not only on its arms but also on its legs, giving it four wings. Scientists think Microraptor could glide between trees and may even have been able to flap and fly short distances. It was a dromaeosaur, a close relative of Velociraptor, and helps show how flight evolved in the dinosaurs that led to birds. Hundreds of specimens are known, many beautifully preserved in fine volcanic ash, with feathers clearly visible. In 2012 scientists studied microscopic structures in its fossil feathers called melanosomes, which hold pigment, and found that Microraptor was glossy black with a blue-green shimmer like a crow or starling.",
s:[["Four wings","The feathers on Microraptor’s legs were long and shaped like flight feathers. Experiments with models in wind tunnels suggest the leg wings helped it glide and steer. It may have held its legs out to the sides or below its body."],["What it ate","Fossils have been found with remains of a bird, a small mammal, a lizard and a fish in their stomachs, showing that Microraptor was a flexible hunter that caught prey in trees, on the ground and near water."],["Color from fossils","Melanosomes come in different shapes for different colors. By comparing those in Microraptor’s feathers with those of living birds, scientists worked out its iridescent black coloring."],["The Jehol Biota","Microraptor comes from the Jehol Biota of Liaoning Province, China, famous for fossils of feathered dinosaurs, early birds, mammals, insects and flowering plants preserved in fine detail."]],
k:[["Lived","About 120 million years ago"],["Where","Liaoning, China"],["Length","About 80 centimeters"],["Weight","About 1 kilogram"],["Group","Dromaeosaur"],["Feather color","Glossy black with a sheen"]],
d:[["2000","Microraptor named"],["2003","Four-winged specimens described"],["2012","Its iridescent black color identified"]],
s2:[["Tiny but fierce","Microraptor had sharp, curved claws and teeth, and a stiffened tail that may have helped it balance and steer in the air."],["Fake fossil","A forged fossil called ‘Archaeoraptor’ in 1999 combined a bird with the tail of a dinosaur later identified as Microraptor."]],
g:[["Dromaeosaur","A group of feathered, sickle-clawed dinosaurs"],["Melanosome","A tiny structure holding pigment"],["Iridescent","Shimmering with changing colors"],["Gliding","Flying without flapping"]],
f:["Microraptor had feathered wings on its legs.","Microraptor was glossy black.","Microraptor lived in China."],
fun:"Microraptor’s iridescent feathers are the oldest evidence of shimmering color in any animal."});

ADDART({id:"terrorbirds",t:"Terror Birds",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦤",w:"Phorusrhacidae",r:["birds","iceagemammals","smilodon","dinobirds","fossils","glyptodon"],
p:[["Giant hunters","Terror birds were flightless predators up to 3 meters tall."],["Hatchet beaks","Their huge hooked beaks were deadly weapons."],["South American rulers","They were top predators in South America for millions of years."]],
l:"Terror birds, known scientifically as phorusrhacids, were large flightless birds that were among the top predators of South America for most of the past 60 million years, after the dinosaurs died out. The biggest, such as Kelenken and Titanis, stood up to about 2 to 3 meters tall, with skulls longer than a horse’s and enormous hooked beaks. They ran on long, powerful legs and probably killed prey by striking with their beaks like a hatchet or by kicking. South America was an island continent for much of this time, and terror birds evolved there with few large mammal predators to compete with. When a land bridge formed with North America about 3 million years ago, animals moved in both directions, and one terror bird, Titanis, reached as far as Texas and Florida. Terror birds became extinct around 2 million years ago or a little later. Their closest living relatives are the seriemas, long-legged birds of South American grasslands that also kill prey by smashing it against the ground.",
s:[["Speed and strength","Studies of their leg bones suggest some smaller terror birds could run at about 50 kilometers per hour. Their skulls were strong from front to back but weak from side to side, so they probably struck downward and quickly pulled back rather than wrestling with prey."],["Kelenken","Kelenken, found in Patagonia and named in 2007, had a skull about 71 centimeters long, the largest of any known bird. It lived about 15 million years ago."],["The Great American Interchange","When the Isthmus of Panama formed, mammals such as big cats, dogs and bears entered South America, while armadillos, ground sloths and terror birds went north. Competition from the new predators may have contributed to terror birds’ decline."],["Living relatives","Seriemas, about 90 centimeters tall, live in Brazil, Argentina and nearby countries. They catch lizards, snakes and rodents and beat them against rocks, much as their giant relatives may have done."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Phorusrhacids"],["Lived","About 60 to 2 million years ago"],["Where","Mostly South America, also North America"],["Height","Up to about 2–3 meters"],["Largest skull","Kelenken, about 71 cm"],["Living relatives","Seriemas"]],
d:[["1887","First terror bird fossils named in Argentina"],["1963","Titanis described from Florida"],["2007","Kelenken named"]],
s2:[["Not dinosaurs","Terror birds were true birds, descended from dinosaurs that survived the asteroid impact 66 million years ago."],["Antarctica too","Fossil terror birds have been found on Seymour Island in Antarctica, from a time when it had forests."]],
g:[["Phorusrhacid","A terror bird"],["Flightless","Unable to fly"],["Seriema","A living relative of terror birds"],["Land bridge","A strip of land connecting two continents"]],
f:["Terror birds could not fly.","Terror birds lived mainly in South America.","Seriemas are their closest living relatives."],
fun:"Kelenken’s skull was longer than the skulls of most horses."});

ADDART({id:"eurypterids",t:"Sea Scorpions",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦂",w:"Eurypterid",r:["trilobites","anomalocaris","cambrian","dunkleosteus","scorpions","fossils"],
p:[["Giant arthropods","Some were longer than an adult person."],["Not true scorpions","They were relatives of scorpions and horseshoe crabs."],["Ancient seas","They lived from about 467 to 252 million years ago."]],
l:"Sea scorpions, or eurypterids, were a group of arthropods, animals with jointed legs and hard outer skeletons, that lived in seas, lagoons and rivers from about 467 to 252 million years ago. They are related to scorpions, spiders and horseshoe crabs, though they were not true scorpions. Most were less than 20 centimeters long, but some grew to enormous sizes: Jaekelopterus, which lived about 390 million years ago in what is now Germany, reached about 2.5 meters, making it one of the largest arthropods that ever lived. Many sea scorpions had a pair of paddle-shaped back legs for swimming and a long tail, sometimes ending in a spike or a flat fin. Some, like Pterygotus and Jaekelopterus, had large grasping claws for catching fish and other prey. They were especially common during the Silurian and Devonian periods, when they were among the top predators in shallow seas. They died out in the great mass extinction at the end of the Permian Period.",
s:[["Giants of the Paleozoic","Jaekelopterus is known from a claw about 46 centimeters long found in 2007. Scientists estimated its full body length at about 2.5 meters. Higher levels of oxygen and a lack of large fish competitors may have allowed such giants."],["Fresh water and land","Many later sea scorpions lived in brackish and fresh water. Fossil trackways suggest some could crawl onto land for short periods, perhaps to mate or move between pools."],["Famous fossils","The Silurian rocks of New York State contain so many sea scorpion fossils that Eurypterus remipes is New York’s official state fossil. Beautiful specimens also come from Estonia and Scotland."],["Relatives today","Horseshoe crabs, which have changed little in hundreds of millions of years, are among sea scorpions’ closest living relatives. Some studies suggest scorpions and sea scorpions may share an ancestor."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Eurypterids"],["Lived","About 467–252 million years ago"],["Largest","Jaekelopterus, about 2.5 meters"],["Relatives","Scorpions, spiders, horseshoe crabs"],["Extinction","End-Permian mass extinction"],["State fossil","New York (Eurypterus)"]],
d:[["1825","Eurypterus described from New York"],["1984","Eurypterus becomes New York’s state fossil"],["2007","Giant Jaekelopterus claw described"]],
s2:[["Swimming paddles","The flat back legs of many sea scorpions worked like oars, letting them swim through the water."],["Eyes","Some sea scorpions had large compound eyes, like insects, that helped them spot prey."]],
g:[["Arthropod","An animal with jointed legs and a hard outer skeleton"],["Eurypterid","A sea scorpion"],["Brackish","Slightly salty water"],["Compound eye","An eye made of many small units"]],
f:["Sea scorpions were not true scorpions.","Some sea scorpions were over 2 meters long.","Sea scorpions died out in the end-Permian extinction."],
fun:"Jaekelopterus’s claw alone was longer than a ruler, about 46 centimeters."});

ADDART({id:"coelacanth",t:"The Coelacanth",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐟",w:"Coelacanth",r:["fishes","tiktaalik","deepsea","fossils","extinction","dunkleosteus"],
p:[["A living fossil","Coelacanths were thought extinct for 66 million years."],["Rediscovered in 1938","A fishing boat caught one off South Africa."],["Lobe fins","Their fleshy fins are related to the limbs of land animals."]],
l:"The coelacanth is a large, rare fish that was known only from fossils, and thought to have died out about 66 million years ago, until a living one was caught off the coast of South Africa in December 1938. Marjorie Courtenay-Latimer, a museum curator in East London, South Africa, spotted the strange blue fish in a fisherman’s catch and contacted the ichthyologist J. L. B. Smith, who identified it and named it Latimeria in her honor. The discovery caused a worldwide sensation. Coelacanths belong to an ancient group called lobe-finned fishes, whose fleshy, limb-like fins are related to the arms and legs of four-legged land animals. Two living species are known: the West Indian Ocean coelacanth, found mainly around the Comoro Islands and southeastern Africa, and the Indonesian coelacanth, discovered in 1997 near Sulawesi. Coelacanths can reach about 2 meters long, live in deep, rocky waters and caves, are active at night, and can live for about 100 years. They are critically endangered or vulnerable.",
s:[["The 1938 discovery","The first fish was caught near the mouth of the Chalumna River. By the time Smith saw it weeks later, its internal organs had been thrown away. He offered a reward for another, and in 1952 a second coelacanth was caught off the Comoro Islands, where local fishers had long known the fish."],["Lobe fins","Coelacanths move their paired fins in alternating patterns, much like the legs of a walking animal. Their fins contain bones that match the arm and leg bones of tetrapods, showing a shared ancestry."],["Slow lives","A 2021 study of growth rings in their scales suggested coelacanths may live about 100 years and do not mature until about age 55. Females carry their young for about five years before giving birth to live pups, one of the longest pregnancies known."],["Not unchanged","Although called living fossils, today’s coelacanths differ from their ancient relatives. Their genome, sequenced in 2013, shows they evolve slowly but have still changed."]],
k:[["Rediscovered","December 1938, South Africa"],["Named after","Marjorie Courtenay-Latimer (Latimeria)"],["Living species","Two (West Indian Ocean and Indonesian)"],["Length","Up to about 2 meters"],["Lifespan","About 100 years"],["Group","Lobe-finned fishes"]],
d:[["1839","First fossil coelacanth described"],["1938","Living coelacanth discovered in South Africa"],["1952","Second specimen caught near the Comoros"],["1997","Indonesian coelacanth discovered"],["2013","Coelacanth genome sequenced"]],
s2:[["Divers","Submersibles and divers have filmed coelacanths drifting head-down in caves 150 to 250 meters deep, almost motionless."],["On a coin","The coelacanth appears on stamps and coins of the Comoros, where it is a national symbol."]],
g:[["Living fossil","A living species very similar to ancient fossil forms"],["Lobe-finned fish","A fish with fleshy, bony fins"],["Ichthyologist","A scientist who studies fish"],["Tetrapod","A four-limbed animal"]],
f:["Coelacanths were thought to be extinct.","Coelacanths have lobe fins.","There are two living species."],
fun:"Coelacanths carry their unborn young for about five years, longer than an elephant’s pregnancy."});

ADDART({id:"titanoboa",t:"Titanoboa",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐍",w:"Titanoboa",r:["snake","climchange","rainforests","fossils","massext","croc"],
p:[["The biggest snake","Titanoboa was about 13 meters long."],["After the dinosaurs","It lived about 60 million years ago."],["A hot world","Its size shows how warm the tropics were then."]],
l:"Titanoboa was a gigantic snake that lived in the tropical rainforests of what is now northern Colombia about 60 million years ago, only a few million years after the extinction of the dinosaurs. It is the largest snake ever found, estimated at about 13 meters long and weighing about 1,100 kilograms, longer than a school bus and much heavier than any living snake. Its fossils were discovered in the Cerrejón coal mine, one of the largest open-pit mines in the world, by a team of Colombian and American scientists, and it was named in 2009. Titanoboa was related to modern boas and anacondas and probably lived much like an anaconda, spending much of its time in rivers and swamps. It likely ate fish, including large lungfish, as well as crocodile relatives and turtles found in the same rocks. Because snakes are cold-blooded and need warmth to grow large, Titanoboa’s size suggests that the tropics were several degrees hotter then than today.",
s:[["Discovery","In 2002, Colombian geology student Edwin Cadena found plant and animal fossils at Cerrejón. Later expeditions with scientists from the Smithsonian Tropical Research Institute and the University of Florida found vertebrae from dozens of giant snakes. The name means ‘titanic boa’."],["Size compared","The largest living snakes, the reticulated python and green anaconda, can reach around 6 to 7 meters and over 200 kilograms. Titanoboa was about twice as long and several times heavier."],["A warm Paleocene","Scientists estimated that average temperatures at Cerrejón needed to be about 30 to 34 degrees Celsius for such a big cold-blooded animal. This estimate has been debated, but other evidence also shows a hot tropical climate."],["The first rainforest","The Cerrejón fossils include the oldest known neotropical rainforest, with plants related to modern beans, palms and bananas, showing that rainforests like the Amazon’s already existed 60 million years ago."]],
k:[["Lived","About 60 million years ago"],["Where","Cerrejón, Colombia"],["Length","About 13 meters"],["Weight","About 1,100 kilograms"],["Relatives","Boas and anacondas"],["Named","2009"]],
d:[["2002","Fossil site at Cerrejón explored"],["2009","Titanoboa named"],["2012","Life-size model displayed in New York"]],
s2:[["Neighbors","Titanoboa shared its rivers with turtles as big as tables and crocodile relatives, some of which it may have eaten."],["How we know its size","Scientists estimate its length from its vertebrae, which are much larger than those of any living snake."]],
g:[["Paleocene","The epoch from about 66 to 56 million years ago"],["Vertebra","A bone of the backbone"],["Cold-blooded","Having a body temperature that depends on surroundings"],["Neotropical","From the tropical Americas"]],
f:["Titanoboa is the largest snake ever found.","Titanoboa lived after the dinosaurs.","Titanoboa was found in Colombia."],
fun:"A Titanoboa would have been so wide at its thickest point that it would have had trouble squeezing through a doorway."});

ADDART({id:"stemcells",t:"Stem Cells",c:"Science",i:"🧫",w:"Stem cell",r:["cell","genetherapy","crispr","transplant","diabetes","dna"],
p:[["Master cells","Stem cells can turn into many kinds of cells."],["Repairing the body","They are already used to treat blood cancers."],["Reprogrammed cells","Ordinary cells can be turned back into stem cells."]],
l:"Stem cells are cells that can divide to make more of themselves and can also develop into specialized cells, such as blood, nerve, muscle or skin cells. They act as the body’s repair system. Embryonic stem cells, from very early embryos, can become almost any cell type. Adult stem cells, found in places such as bone marrow, skin and the gut, replace cells that wear out but usually make only a few types. In 2006 the Japanese scientist Shinya Yamanaka discovered how to turn ordinary adult cells back into stem cells by switching on just four genes, creating induced pluripotent stem cells (iPS cells), a discovery that won the 2012 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. The best-established stem cell treatment is the bone marrow or blood stem cell transplant, used since the 1950s and 60s to treat leukemia and other blood diseases. Researchers are testing stem cell treatments for diabetes, Parkinson’s disease, eye diseases and heart damage, but many advertised ‘stem cell cures’ are unproven and can be dangerous.",
s:[["Kinds of stem cells","Totipotent cells, from the first divisions of a fertilized egg, can form a whole organism. Pluripotent cells, such as embryonic stem cells and iPS cells, can form almost any body cell. Multipotent adult stem cells make a limited range of cells, such as all the types of blood cells."],["Bone marrow transplants","Blood stem cells live in bone marrow. In a transplant, a patient’s diseased marrow is destroyed with drugs or radiation and replaced with healthy stem cells from a donor or from the patient. The American doctor E. Donnall Thomas pioneered this and won a Nobel Prize in 1990. Registries list tens of millions of volunteer donors worldwide."],["New treatments","In 2025 researchers reported in the New England Journal of Medicine that stem cell–derived insulin-making cells allowed most of a small group of people with severe type 1 diabetes to stop insulin injections. Trials are also testing stem cell–derived nerve cells for Parkinson’s disease and retina cells for eye diseases."],["Ethics and safety","Using human embryos for research raises ethical debates, and rules differ by country. iPS cells avoid some of these concerns. Doctors warn against unregulated clinics selling unproven stem cell injections, which have caused serious harm."]],
k:[["Can become","Many kinds of specialized cells"],["Types","Embryonic, adult, induced pluripotent"],["iPS cells discovered","2006, Shinya Yamanaka"],["Established treatment","Blood stem cell (bone marrow) transplants"],["Nobel Prizes","1990 (Thomas), 2012 (Yamanaka and Gurdon)"]],
d:[["1957","E. Donnall Thomas reports early bone marrow transplants"],["1998","Human embryonic stem cells grown in the lab"],["2006","Induced pluripotent stem cells created"],["2012","Nobel Prize for reprogramming cells"],["2025","Stem cell–derived islet results published for type 1 diabetes"]],
s2:[["Cord blood","Blood from the umbilical cord after birth contains blood stem cells and can be donated and stored for transplants."],["Organoids","Scientists grow tiny, simplified organs called organoids from stem cells to study diseases and test drugs."]],
g:[["Stem cell","A cell that can renew itself and become other cell types"],["Pluripotent","Able to become almost any cell type"],["Bone marrow","Soft tissue inside bones where blood cells are made"],["Organoid","A tiny lab-grown organ model"]],
f:["Stem cells can become other cell types.","Bone marrow transplants use stem cells.","Adult cells can be reprogrammed into stem cells."],
fun:"Your body makes roughly 2 million new red blood cells every second, all from stem cells in your bone marrow."});

ADDART({id:"imfworldbank",t:"The IMF and the World Bank",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🏦",w:"International Monetary Fund",r:["un","banks","trade","inflation","interestdebt","ww2"],
p:[["Born in 1944","Both were created at a conference in Bretton Woods, USA."],["Two different jobs","The IMF steadies the world’s money system; the World Bank funds development."],["Nearly every country","Each has about 190 member countries."]],
l:"The International Monetary Fund (IMF) and the World Bank are international organizations created at the Bretton Woods Conference in New Hampshire, United States, in July 1944, as world leaders planned how to rebuild the global economy after the Second World War. Both are based in Washington, D.C., and each has about 190 member countries. The IMF works to keep the international money system stable: it watches countries’ economies, gives advice, and lends money to countries in financial crisis, usually on the condition that they make economic changes. The World Bank lends money and gives grants to developing countries for long-term projects such as roads, schools, clean water, electricity and health care, with the goal of ending extreme poverty. By tradition, the head of the IMF has been European and the head of the World Bank American. In 2026 the IMF was led by Kristalina Georgieva of Bulgaria and the World Bank by Ajay Banga, who was born in India and is an American citizen. Both institutions have been praised for helping in crises and criticized for the conditions attached to their loans.",
s:[["Bretton Woods","Delegates from 44 countries met at a hotel in Bretton Woods in 1944. Economists John Maynard Keynes of Britain and Harry Dexter White of the United States led the design. The system they created also tied currencies to the US dollar, which was linked to gold until 1971."],["How the IMF works","Member countries contribute money according to the size of their economies, which also decides their voting power. The IMF’s largest loans have gone to countries such as Argentina, Greece and Ukraine. It also issues a reserve asset called Special Drawing Rights."],["The World Bank Group","The World Bank Group includes the International Bank for Reconstruction and Development, which lends to middle-income countries, and the International Development Association, which gives low-cost loans and grants to the poorest countries. Its first loan, in 1947, went to France for postwar rebuilding."],["Debates","Critics say IMF loan conditions, such as cutting government spending, have sometimes hurt poor people, and that richer countries have too much voting power. Supporters say the institutions prevent crises from spreading and fund vital projects. Both have reformed some policies over time."]],
k:[["Founded","1944, Bretton Woods Conference"],["Headquarters","Washington, D.C."],["Members","About 190 countries each"],["IMF’s job","Stability of the world money system"],["World Bank’s job","Loans and grants for development"],["Leaders (2026)","Kristalina Georgieva (IMF), Ajay Banga (World Bank)"]],
d:[["1944","Bretton Woods Conference"],["1945","IMF and World Bank founded"],["1947","World Bank’s first loan, to France"],["1971","Dollar–gold link ends"],["2023","Ajay Banga becomes World Bank president"]],
s2:[["Extreme poverty","The World Bank measures extreme poverty using an international poverty line, updated in 2025 to $3.00 per person per day."],["Spring and Annual Meetings","Finance ministers and central bank chiefs from around the world meet twice a year at the IMF and World Bank meetings."]],
g:[["Bretton Woods","The 1944 conference that set up the IMF and World Bank"],["Loan conditions","Changes a country agrees to make to receive a loan"],["Developing country","A country with lower average incomes"],["Reserve asset","Money that governments hold in reserve"]],
f:["The IMF and World Bank were founded after the Bretton Woods Conference.","Both are based in Washington, D.C.","The World Bank funds development projects."],
fun:"The Mount Washington Hotel where the Bretton Woods Conference met still welcomes guests, and the meeting room is preserved."});

ADDART({id:"redcross",t:"The Red Cross and Red Crescent",c:"Economics and Society",i:"➕",w:"International Red Cross and Red Crescent Movement",r:["un","wwi","firstaid","migration","nobel"],
p:[["Helping in war and disaster","The movement aids people affected by conflict and emergencies."],["Three emblems","The red cross, red crescent and red crystal."],["Nobel Prizes","It has won the Nobel Peace Prize three times."]],
l:"The International Red Cross and Red Crescent Movement is the world’s largest humanitarian network, helping people affected by war, natural disasters and health emergencies. It began after the Swiss businessman Henry Dunant witnessed thousands of wounded soldiers left without care after the Battle of Solferino in Italy in 1859. In his book A Memory of Solferino, he called for volunteer relief societies and an international agreement to protect the wounded. This led to the founding of the International Committee of the Red Cross (ICRC) in Geneva in 1863 and the first Geneva Convention in 1864. Today the movement has three parts: the ICRC, which works in armed conflicts, visits prisoners of war and helps reunite families; the International Federation of Red Cross and Red Crescent Societies, which coordinates disaster relief; and about 190 national societies with millions of volunteers. Its emblems, the red cross, the red crescent and, since 2005, the red crystal, are protected under international law and must not be attacked. It is guided by principles including humanity, neutrality and impartiality.",
s:[["Henry Dunant","Dunant shared the first Nobel Peace Prize in 1901. The ICRC itself won the prize in 1917 and 1944 for its work during the two world wars, and shared it with the Federation in 1963."],["The Geneva Conventions","The Geneva Conventions, updated in 1949, set rules for war: wounded soldiers, prisoners, medical workers and civilians must be protected. Almost every country in the world has signed them. The ICRC works to remind armed groups of these rules."],["Three emblems","The red cross on white is the reverse of the Swiss flag. The red crescent has been used since the 1870s, mainly in Muslim-majority countries. The red crystal was added in 2005 as an emblem free of any religious or national meaning."],["The seven principles","The movement follows seven fundamental principles: humanity, impartiality, neutrality, independence, voluntary service, unity and universality. Neutrality allows it to work on all sides of a conflict."]],
k:[["Founded","1863, Geneva"],["Founder","Henry Dunant"],["Emblems","Red cross, red crescent, red crystal"],["National societies","About 190"],["Nobel Peace Prizes","1917, 1944, 1963 (plus Dunant in 1901)"],["World Red Cross Day","8 May (Dunant’s birthday)"]],
d:[["1859","Battle of Solferino"],["1863","ICRC founded"],["1864","First Geneva Convention"],["1919","League of Red Cross Societies founded"],["1949","Geneva Conventions updated"],["2005","Red crystal emblem adopted"]],
s2:[["Tracing families","The ICRC’s Central Tracing Agency has helped families find missing relatives since the Franco-Prussian War in 1870."],["Blood donation","In many countries, the national Red Cross or Red Crescent society runs blood donation services."]],
g:[["Humanitarian","Concerned with helping people in need"],["Geneva Conventions","International treaties protecting people in war"],["Neutrality","Not taking sides"],["Emblem","A protected symbol"]],
f:["The Red Cross was founded in Geneva.","Henry Dunant inspired the Red Cross.","The red crystal is one of its emblems."],
fun:"The red cross emblem was created by reversing the colors of the flag of Switzerland, Dunant’s home country."});

ADDART({id:"g20brics",t:"The G20 and BRICS",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🌐",w:"G20",r:["un","eu","trade","imfworldbank","china","indiacountry"],
p:[["Clubs of big economies","Both bring together major countries to discuss the world economy."],["The G20","19 countries plus the European Union and the African Union."],["BRICS","A group led by Brazil, Russia, India, China and South Africa that has grown."]],
l:"The G20 and BRICS are groups of countries whose leaders meet regularly to discuss the world economy and global issues. The G20, or Group of Twenty, was created in 1999 after an Asian financial crisis and became a meeting of heads of government during the 2008 global financial crisis. Its members are 19 countries, Argentina, Australia, Brazil, Canada, China, France, Germany, India, Indonesia, Italy, Japan, Mexico, Russia, Saudi Arabia, South Africa, South Korea, Türkiye, the United Kingdom and the United States, plus the European Union and, since 2023, the African Union. Together they make up about 85 percent of the world economy. BRICS began as an idea in 2001, when an economist grouped Brazil, Russia, India and China as fast-growing economies; their leaders first met in 2009, and South Africa joined in 2010. BRICS expanded from 2024, adding Egypt, Ethiopia, Iran, the United Arab Emirates and, in 2025, Indonesia, with Saudi Arabia invited, plus a set of partner countries. Neither group can make binding laws; their influence comes from agreements among powerful members.",
s:[["How the G20 works","The G20 has no permanent office. Each year a different member holds the presidency and hosts the leaders’ summit. India hosted in 2023, Brazil in 2024 and South Africa in 2025, the first summit in Africa. The United States holds the 2026 presidency, with a summit planned for December 2026 in Miami, and did not invite South Africa’s government to take part."],["The origin of BRICS","In 2001 Jim O’Neill, an economist at the bank Goldman Sachs, coined the term BRIC for four large emerging economies. The countries turned the idea into a real group, holding their first summit in Russia in 2009. The S was added when South Africa joined in 2010."],["New Development Bank","In 2015 BRICS countries launched the New Development Bank, based in Shanghai, to fund infrastructure and sustainable development projects. Brazil’s former president Dilma Rousseff became its head in 2023."],["Different views","Supporters see BRICS as giving a stronger voice to developing countries. Critics say its members have very different interests and political systems. The G20 is praised for bringing together both rich and emerging economies but criticized for reaching few concrete agreements."]],
k:[["G20 founded","1999 (leaders’ summits since 2008)"],["G20 members","19 countries, the EU and the African Union"],["G20 share of world economy","About 85 percent"],["BRICS first summit","2009"],["BRICS 2026 summit","New Delhi, India"],["BRICS bank","New Development Bank (2015)"]],
d:[["1999","G20 created"],["2001","The term BRIC is coined"],["2008","First G20 leaders’ summit"],["2009","First BRIC summit"],["2010","South Africa joins"],["2023","African Union joins the G20"],["2024","BRICS expands"],["2025","Indonesia joins BRICS"]],
s2:[["G7","The G7 is a smaller group of advanced economies: Canada, France, Germany, Italy, Japan, the United Kingdom and the United States, with the European Union also taking part."],["Family photo","Every G20 summit ends with a ‘family photo’ of all the leaders, a tradition watched closely for who stands where."]],
g:[["Summit","A meeting of heads of government"],["Presidency","The member that leads a group for a year"],["Emerging economy","A fast-growing developing economy"],["Binding","Legally required to follow"]],
f:["The African Union joined the G20 in 2023.","BRICS started with Brazil, Russia, India and China.","Neither group makes binding laws."],
fun:"The name BRIC was coined in a 2001 research paper long before the countries ever met as a group."});

ADDART({id:"fractals",t:"Fractals",c:"Mathematics",i:"❄️",w:"Fractal",r:["shapes","infin","euclid","chaostheory","prob"],
p:[["Patterns within patterns","Fractals look similar when you zoom in."],["Everywhere in nature","Coastlines, ferns, clouds and lungs are fractal-like."],["Simple rules","Endlessly complex shapes come from simple steps repeated."]],
l:"A fractal is a shape or pattern that repeats itself at smaller and smaller scales, so that zooming in on part of it reveals a copy, or near-copy, of the whole. This property is called self-similarity. Fractals can be made by repeating a simple rule over and over: for example, the Koch snowflake is made by adding a smaller triangle to the middle of every edge of a triangle, again and again, producing an infinitely long, crinkly outline around a finite area. The word fractal was coined in 1975 by the Polish-born French-American mathematician Benoît Mandelbrot, who showed that many rough, irregular shapes in nature that ordinary geometry could not describe, such as coastlines, mountains, clouds and branching trees, can be studied as fractals. The most famous fractal, the Mandelbrot set, produced by a short formula and a computer, has an infinitely detailed boundary full of spirals and miniature copies of itself. Fractals are used in computer graphics, antennas, medicine and the study of natural patterns.",
s:[["Fractal dimension","Fractals can have dimensions that are not whole numbers. A smooth line has dimension 1 and a flat area dimension 2, but the Koch snowflake’s outline has a dimension of about 1.26, showing it is more crinkly than a line but does not fill a surface."],["How long is a coastline?","Mandelbrot’s 1967 paper asked how long the coast of Britain is. The answer depends on the length of your measuring stick: the shorter it is, the more bays and wiggles you capture and the longer the coast appears."],["Fractals in nature","Ferns, Romanesco broccoli, river networks, lightning, blood vessels and the branching airways of the lungs all have fractal-like patterns. Branching lets lungs pack a huge surface area for breathing into the chest."],["Uses","Movie and game designers generate realistic landscapes, clouds and trees with fractal formulas. Fractal-shaped antennas in mobile phones can pick up many frequencies in a small space."]],
k:[["Key property","Self-similarity"],["Word coined","1975, by Benoît Mandelbrot"],["Famous examples","Mandelbrot set, Koch snowflake, Sierpiński triangle"],["Koch snowflake dimension","About 1.26"],["In nature","Coastlines, ferns, lungs, lightning"]],
d:[["1904","Helge von Koch describes the Koch snowflake"],["1915","Wacław Sierpiński describes his triangle"],["1975","Mandelbrot coins the word fractal"],["1980","Mandelbrot set first plotted in detail by computer"]],
s2:[["Romanesco","Romanesco broccoli is a striking natural fractal: each bud is made of smaller buds arranged in spirals."],["Infinite zoom","Computer videos zooming into the Mandelbrot set can magnify it trillions of times and still find new detail."]],
g:[["Fractal","A shape with similar patterns at every scale"],["Self-similarity","Looking alike at different scales"],["Iteration","Repeating a step many times"],["Dimension","A measure of how a shape fills space"]],
f:["Fractals repeat at smaller scales.","Benoît Mandelbrot named fractals.","Coastlines are fractal-like."],
fun:"The Koch snowflake has a finite area but an outline of infinite length."});

ADDART({id:"gametheory",t:"Game Theory",c:"Mathematics",i:"♟️",w:"Game theory",r:["prob","stats","money","logic","evolution"],
p:[["The math of decisions","It studies how people choose when their choices affect each other."],["Prisoner’s dilemma","A famous puzzle about trust and cooperation."],["Used everywhere","From economics and politics to biology."]],
l:"Game theory is the branch of mathematics that studies strategic decisions, situations in which the outcome for each person depends not only on their own choices but also on the choices of others. Despite its name, it is about far more than games: it is used to understand business competition, auctions, elections, negotiations, international relations, traffic and even the behavior of animals and genes. Game theory was founded as a field by the mathematician John von Neumann and the economist Oskar Morgenstern in their 1944 book Theory of Games and Economic Behavior. In 1950 the American mathematician John Nash introduced the idea now called a Nash equilibrium: a situation in which no player can do better by changing their strategy alone, given what the others are doing. The most famous example is the prisoner’s dilemma, which shows how two people acting in their own interest can end up worse off than if they had cooperated. Several Nobel Prizes in economics have been awarded for work in game theory, including to Nash in 1994.",
s:[["The prisoner’s dilemma","Two suspects are questioned separately. If both stay silent, both get a short sentence. If one betrays the other, the betrayer goes free and the other gets a long sentence. If both betray, both get medium sentences. Each is tempted to betray, so both often end up worse off than if they had trusted each other."],["Repeated games","When a game is played many times, cooperation can win. In computer tournaments run by Robert Axelrod in 1980, a simple strategy called ‘tit for tat’, which cooperates first and then copies the other player’s last move, did remarkably well."],["Zero-sum and non-zero-sum","In zero-sum games, like chess, one player’s gain is the other’s loss. Many real situations are non-zero-sum: both sides can gain by cooperating, as in trade, or both can lose, as in an arms race."],["Biology","The biologist John Maynard Smith applied game theory to evolution in the 1970s, explaining why animals often settle contests with displays rather than deadly fights. Strategies that are hard to beat become common."]],
k:[["Studies","Strategic decisions among several players"],["Founded","1944, von Neumann and Morgenstern"],["Key idea","Nash equilibrium (1950)"],["Famous example","Prisoner’s dilemma"],["Nobel Prizes","1994 (Nash, Harsanyi, Selten) and others"]],
d:[["1928","Von Neumann proves the minimax theorem"],["1944","Theory of Games and Economic Behavior"],["1950","Nash describes his equilibrium; prisoner’s dilemma devised"],["1980","Axelrod’s tournaments"],["1994","Nobel Prize for game theory"]],
s2:[["A Beautiful Mind","John Nash’s life and struggle with schizophrenia were told in the book and the 2001 film A Beautiful Mind."],["Auctions","Game theory has been used to design auctions for selling radio frequencies, which have raised billions of dollars for governments."]],
g:[["Strategy","A plan for what to do in every situation"],["Nash equilibrium","A state where no one gains by changing strategy alone"],["Zero-sum game","A game where one side’s gain is the other’s loss"],["Payoff","The result a player gets"]],
f:["Game theory studies strategic decisions.","John Nash described the Nash equilibrium.","Tit for tat can encourage cooperation."],
fun:"Rock, paper, scissors has no winning strategy: the best plan is to choose randomly."});

ADDART({id:"chaostheory",t:"Chaos Theory",c:"Mathematics",i:"🦋",w:"Chaos theory",r:["climate","fractals","prob","newton","particles"],
p:[["Tiny changes, big effects","Small differences can grow into huge ones."],["The butterfly effect","A butterfly’s wings could, in theory, change the weather weeks later."],["Order in disorder","Chaotic systems still follow exact rules."]],
l:"Chaos theory is the study of systems that follow exact rules yet behave in ways that are practically impossible to predict far ahead, because tiny differences in their starting conditions grow rapidly into huge differences later. Such systems are called chaotic. They include the weather, the swirling of fluids, a double pendulum, the orbits of some asteroids and many populations of animals. Chaos was first glimpsed by the French mathematician Henri Poincaré in the 1880s, but the modern field began in 1961 when the American meteorologist Edward Lorenz, running a simple weather model on a computer, re-entered a number rounded from six to three decimal places and found that the forecast soon became completely different. He later described this as the ‘butterfly effect’: the idea that the flap of a butterfly’s wings in one place could, in principle, set off a chain of events leading to a tornado elsewhere. Chaos explains why weather forecasts become unreliable beyond about two weeks. Chaotic systems are not random, however: they often form intricate, fractal patterns called strange attractors.",
s:[["Lorenz’s discovery","Lorenz rounded 0.506127 to 0.506 and expected nearly the same result. Instead, the two simulated weather patterns quickly diverged. In 1972 he gave a talk titled ‘Predictability: Does the Flap of a Butterfly’s Wings in Brazil Set Off a Tornado in Texas?’"],["Strange attractors","When plotted, the behavior of Lorenz’s weather model traces a looping shape like a butterfly’s wings, called the Lorenz attractor. The path never repeats exactly but stays within the same shape, combining order and unpredictability."],["The double pendulum","A pendulum hanging from another pendulum is a simple example of chaos. Start two double pendulums from almost the same position and within seconds they swing in completely different ways."],["Living with chaos","Weather forecasters run many forecasts with slightly different starting conditions, called an ensemble, to estimate the chances of different outcomes. Chaos theory also helps scientists understand heart rhythms, population booms and crashes, and turbulence."]],
k:[["Key idea","Sensitive dependence on initial conditions"],["Popular name","The butterfly effect"],["Modern field began","1961, Edward Lorenz"],["Early pioneer","Henri Poincaré (1880s)"],["Weather forecast limit","About two weeks"]],
d:[["1887","Poincaré studies the unpredictable three-body problem"],["1961","Lorenz’s rounding discovery"],["1963","Lorenz publishes his weather model"],["1972","Lorenz’s butterfly lecture"],["1975","The word chaos is used in this sense"]],
s2:[["Not random","A chaotic system is deterministic: if you knew its starting state perfectly, you could predict it. The problem is that we can never measure anything perfectly."],["Three-body problem","Three objects pulling on each other by gravity, such as three stars, can move chaotically, as Poincaré showed."]],
g:[["Chaos","Unpredictable behavior from exact rules"],["Butterfly effect","Tiny changes leading to big effects"],["Strange attractor","A complex pattern a chaotic system follows"],["Ensemble forecast","Many forecasts run with slightly different starts"]],
f:["Chaotic systems follow exact rules.","Edward Lorenz discovered the butterfly effect.","Chaos limits weather forecasts."],
fun:"Lorenz first used a seagull, not a butterfly, to describe the effect; the butterfly came from the title of his 1972 talk."});

ADDART({id:"temperature",t:"Temperature and Thermometers",c:"Science",i:"🌡️",w:"Temperature",r:["thermo","atom","climate","fever","weatherforecast"],
p:[["Hot and cold measured","Temperature measures how fast particles move."],["Three main scales","Celsius, Fahrenheit and Kelvin."],["Absolute zero","Nothing can be colder than −273.15 °C."]],
l:"Temperature is a measure of how hot or cold something is. At the level of atoms and molecules, it reflects how fast the particles are moving and vibrating: the faster they move, the higher the temperature. Heat is the energy that flows from a hotter object to a colder one until they reach the same temperature. Temperature is measured with thermometers, which have been made in many ways: liquids such as alcohol that expand when heated, metal strips that bend, electronic sensors whose resistance changes, and infrared devices that read the light objects give off. Three temperature scales are in common use. The Celsius scale, used in most countries, sets 0 degrees at the freezing point of water and 100 at its boiling point at sea level. The Fahrenheit scale, used mainly in the United States, puts these at 32 and 212 degrees. Scientists use the Kelvin scale, which starts at absolute zero, −273.15 °C, the coldest possible temperature, at which particles have the least possible energy.",
s:[["Inventing the thermometer","Galileo Galilei made an early ‘thermoscope’ around 1593 that showed temperature changes but had no scale. Daniel Gabriel Fahrenheit made reliable mercury thermometers and his scale in the 1710s and 1720s. Anders Celsius proposed his scale in 1742, though he originally put 0 at boiling and 100 at freezing."],["Converting","To change Celsius to Fahrenheit, multiply by 9/5 and add 32. So 20 °C is 68 °F, and body temperature of about 37 °C is about 98.6 °F. To get kelvins, add 273.15 to the Celsius temperature. The two scales meet at −40, where −40 °C equals −40 °F."],["Extremes","The coldest temperature measured on Earth’s surface was about −89.2 °C at Vostok Station in Antarctica in 1983. Laboratories have cooled atoms to within billionths of a degree of absolute zero. The center of the Sun is about 15 million °C."],["Body temperature","Normal human body temperature is about 37 °C, though it varies slightly between people and through the day. Doctors use thermometers to check for fever, which is usually 38 °C or higher."]],
k:[["Measures","How fast particles move"],["Water freezes","0 °C, 32 °F, 273.15 K"],["Water boils (sea level)","100 °C, 212 °F, 373.15 K"],["Absolute zero","−273.15 °C, 0 K"],["Scales meet","−40 °C = −40 °F"]],
d:[["c. 1593","Galileo’s thermoscope"],["1714","Fahrenheit’s mercury thermometer"],["1742","Celsius proposes his scale"],["1848","Lord Kelvin proposes an absolute scale"],["1983","Record low of −89.2 °C at Vostok Station"]],
s2:[["Feels like","On a windy or humid day, the ‘feels like’ temperature can differ from the real air temperature because wind and humidity change how quickly our bodies lose heat."],["Infrared thermometers","Thermometers aimed at the forehead measure infrared light given off by the skin without touching it."]],
g:[["Temperature","A measure of how hot or cold something is"],["Heat","Energy flowing from hot to cold"],["Kelvin","The scientific temperature scale starting at absolute zero"],["Absolute zero","The lowest possible temperature"]],
f:["Water freezes at 0 °C.","Absolute zero is −273.15 °C.","−40 °C equals −40 °F."],
fun:"Anders Celsius’s original scale was upside down: water boiled at 0 and froze at 100."});

ADDART({id:"entropy",t:"Entropy",c:"Science",i:"🔀",w:"Entropy",r:["thermo","temperature","prob","fateuniverse","energyphys","atom"],
p:[["A measure of disorder","Entropy measures how spread out energy is."],["Always increasing","In an isolated system, entropy tends to grow."],["The arrow of time","Entropy helps explain why time seems to flow one way."]],
l:"Entropy is a measure of how spread out or disordered energy is in a system, or, more precisely, of how many different ways the tiny parts of a system can be arranged while looking the same from the outside. The idea was introduced in the 1850s and 1860s by the German physicist Rudolf Clausius, who coined the word in 1865, and explained in terms of atoms by Ludwig Boltzmann in the 1870s. The second law of thermodynamics states that the total entropy of an isolated system never decreases: things naturally go from ordered to disordered states. A hot cup of coffee cools down as its heat spreads into the room, an ice cube melts in a warm drink, and a dropped egg breaks, but these never happen in reverse on their own. That one-way direction is why entropy is linked to the ‘arrow of time’. Living things and machines can create order locally, such as a refrigerator making ice, but only by producing even more disorder elsewhere, usually as waste heat. Entropy also appears in information theory, where it measures uncertainty.",
s:[["Counting arrangements","Boltzmann showed that a state with more possible arrangements of its particles is more likely. There are vastly more ways for gas molecules to be spread through a room than packed into one corner, so gas spreads out and never gathers back by itself. His formula, S = k log W, is carved on his gravestone in Vienna."],["The second law","The second law explains why perpetual motion machines are impossible and why no engine can turn heat completely into work. Some energy is always lost as waste heat."],["Life and order","Plants and animals build ordered bodies, but they do so by using energy from sunlight or food and releasing heat and simpler molecules, increasing total entropy. Earth receives low-entropy sunlight and radiates high-entropy heat into space."],["Information entropy","In 1948 the American engineer Claude Shannon defined entropy for information, measuring how unpredictable a message is. His idea underlies data compression, such as ZIP files and streaming video."]],
k:[["Measures","Disorder, or how spread out energy is"],["Word coined","1865, Rudolf Clausius"],["Explained with atoms","Ludwig Boltzmann, 1870s"],["Second law","Entropy of an isolated system never decreases"],["Information entropy","Claude Shannon, 1948"]],
d:[["1824","Sadi Carnot studies the efficiency of engines"],["1865","Clausius coins entropy"],["1877","Boltzmann links entropy to probability"],["1948","Shannon’s information entropy"]],
s2:[["Heat death","Some scientists have suggested that, in the far future, the universe could reach maximum entropy, a ‘heat death’ in which no useful energy remains."],["Your room","A messy room is a common everyday example of entropy, though physicists point out that tidiness is not exactly the same as thermodynamic order."]],
g:[["Entropy","A measure of disorder or spread-out energy"],["Second law of thermodynamics","Total entropy tends to increase"],["Isolated system","A system that exchanges no energy with its surroundings"],["Arrow of time","The one-way direction of time"]],
f:["Entropy tends to increase.","Rudolf Clausius coined the word entropy.","Entropy is linked to the arrow of time."],
fun:"Boltzmann’s entropy formula is engraved on his tombstone in Vienna."});

ADDART({id:"anesthesia",t:"Anesthesia",c:"Health",i:"💉",w:"Anesthesia",r:["surgery","vacc","nervous","brain","pain"],
p:[["Surgery without pain","Anesthesia blocks pain during operations."],["General and local","Some anesthetics put you to sleep; others numb one area."],["A 19th-century breakthrough","Ether was first used publicly in 1846."]],
l:"Anesthesia is the use of medicines to prevent pain and awareness during surgery and other medical procedures. Before anesthesia, operations had to be done as fast as possible on patients who were awake and in agony, and many people avoided surgery altogether. General anesthesia puts a patient into a controlled, sleep-like state in which they feel nothing and remember nothing; it is given as gases breathed through a mask or tube, or as drugs injected into a vein. Local anesthesia numbs a small area, such as a tooth, while the patient stays awake. Regional anesthesia, such as an epidural or spinal block, numbs a larger part of the body, often used during childbirth. Sedation helps patients relax and feel drowsy during smaller procedures. Doctors who specialize in anesthesia, called anesthesiologists or anesthetists, monitor breathing, heart rate, blood pressure and oxygen levels throughout. Modern anesthesia is very safe, with serious problems now rare. Scientists still do not fully understand exactly how general anesthetics switch off consciousness.",
s:[["Ether Day","On 16 October 1846, the American dentist William Morton gave ether to a patient at Massachusetts General Hospital in Boston while a surgeon removed a growth from his neck. The patient felt no pain. The event, remembered as Ether Day, spread news of anesthesia around the world within months."],["Earlier pioneers","In Japan in 1804, the surgeon Hanaoka Seishū used an herbal anesthetic called tsūsensan to remove a breast tumor. In the United States, Crawford Long used ether in 1842 but did not publish his work until later. Nitrous oxide, or laughing gas, was tried by the dentist Horace Wells in 1845."],["Chloroform and a queen","Chloroform was introduced in 1847 by the Scottish doctor James Young Simpson. Its use became widely accepted after Queen Victoria was given chloroform while giving birth in 1853."],["How it works","Local anesthetics such as lidocaine block nerves from sending pain signals. General anesthetics act on proteins in nerve cells in the brain and spinal cord, reducing communication between brain regions. Researchers continue to study how this turns off awareness."]],
k:[["Purpose","Preventing pain and awareness during procedures"],["Types","General, regional, local, sedation"],["First public demonstration","16 October 1846, Boston"],["Early anesthetics","Ether, nitrous oxide, chloroform"],["Specialists","Anesthesiologists or anesthetists"]],
d:[["1804","Hanaoka Seishū operates under herbal anesthesia"],["1842","Crawford Long uses ether"],["1846","Ether Day in Boston"],["1847","Chloroform introduced"],["1884","Cocaine used as a local anesthetic in eye surgery"]],
s2:[["Waking up","After general anesthesia, patients usually wake within minutes of the drugs being stopped, though they may feel groggy for a few hours."],["Fasting","Patients are usually asked not to eat for several hours before general anesthesia, because food in the stomach could be breathed into the lungs while unconscious."]],
g:[["Anesthesia","Loss of sensation or awareness caused by medicine"],["General anesthesia","A controlled unconscious state"],["Local anesthesia","Numbing of a small area"],["Epidural","An injection that numbs the lower body"]],
f:["General anesthesia makes patients unconscious.","Ether was first demonstrated publicly in 1846.","Local anesthesia numbs a small area."],
fun:"The word anesthesia was suggested in 1846 by the writer and doctor Oliver Wendell Holmes Sr., from Greek words meaning ‘without sensation’."});

ADDART({id:"puberty",t:"Puberty and Growing Up",c:"Health",i:"🌱",w:"Puberty",r:["hormone","brain","skin","sleep","nutri"],
p:[["Becoming an adult","Puberty is when the body changes from a child’s to an adult’s."],["Driven by hormones","Hormones from the brain and glands trigger the changes."],["Everyone’s timing differs","It usually starts between about 8 and 14."]],
l:"Puberty is the stage of life when a child’s body develops into an adult’s body and becomes able to reproduce. It usually begins between about 8 and 13 years of age in girls and about 9 and 14 in boys, and lasts several years, though the timing varies widely and is different for everyone. Puberty is started by the brain: a region called the hypothalamus signals the pituitary gland, which in turn signals the ovaries or testes to make more sex hormones, mainly estrogen and testosterone. These hormones cause many changes, including a growth spurt, when young people may grow 8 to 10 centimeters a year, the growth of body hair, sweat glands becoming more active, skin changes such as acne, voice changes, and the development of features such as breasts or broader shoulders. Girls begin to have menstrual periods. The brain also keeps developing throughout the teenage years, especially areas involved in planning, decision-making and emotions, which is one reason feelings can be intense during this time. Puberty is a normal and healthy part of growing up.",
s:[["The growth spurt","During puberty, bones lengthen quickly, then the growth plates at their ends close and growth stops. Girls usually have their growth spurt earlier than boys, which is why girls are often taller than boys around age 11 or 12. Most people reach their adult height by their late teens."],["Skin and sweat","Hormones make oil glands in the skin more active, which can cause pimples. Sweat glands under the arms also become active, so body odor can appear. Washing regularly and, if needed, using deodorant helps."],["The teenage brain","Brain scans show that the teenage brain is reorganizing, strengthening useful connections and trimming unused ones. The prefrontal cortex, which helps with planning and self-control, keeps developing into the mid-twenties."],["Looking after yourself","Sleep, healthy food and physical activity help the body through its rapid changes. Teenagers need about 8 to 10 hours of sleep a night. Talking with a parent, trusted adult, school nurse or doctor can help with questions or worries."]],
k:[["Typical start","About 8–13 in girls, 9–14 in boys"],["Started by","The hypothalamus and pituitary gland in the brain"],["Main hormones","Estrogen and testosterone"],["Growth spurt","Up to about 8–10 cm a year"],["Teen sleep need","About 8–10 hours a night"]],
s2:[["Voice changes","As the voice box grows during puberty, especially in boys, the voice deepens. For a while, it may crack or change pitch unexpectedly."],["Different timing","Starting puberty earlier or later than friends is common and usually normal. A doctor can check if it seems very early or very late."]],
g:[["Puberty","The change from a child’s body to an adult’s"],["Hormone","A chemical messenger in the body"],["Pituitary gland","A small gland at the base of the brain that controls other glands"],["Growth plate","The area near the end of a bone where growth happens"]],
f:["Puberty is started by signals from the brain.","Puberty timing varies between people.","The brain keeps developing through the teenage years."],
fun:"During the peak of the growth spurt, some teenagers grow more than a centimeter in a single month."});

ADDART({id:"placebo",t:"The Placebo Effect",c:"Health",i:"💊",w:"Placebo",r:["vacc","brain","pain","stress","psychology"],
p:[["Fake pills, real effects","People can feel better after taking a treatment with no active ingredient."],["Expectation matters","Belief that a treatment will work can change how we feel."],["Essential for testing","New medicines are compared against placebos."]],
l:"A placebo is a treatment that has no active medical ingredient, such as a sugar pill, a salt-water injection or a pretend procedure. The placebo effect is the improvement some people feel after receiving one, simply because they expect to get better. Placebos can reduce how much pain, nausea, tiredness or anxiety people feel, especially symptoms that the brain helps control. They do not shrink tumors, cure infections or fix broken bones, but they can change how people experience their symptoms. Scientists think the effect works through expectation and learning: the brain anticipates relief and may release natural painkillers called endorphins and other chemicals. Even the color, price and size of a pill can change its effect, and in some studies people felt better even when told they were taking a placebo. The opposite, called the nocebo effect, happens when people expect side effects and feel them from a harmless treatment. Because the placebo effect is so common, new medicines are tested in clinical trials that compare them with placebos, often in ‘double-blind’ studies where neither patients nor doctors know who gets which.",
s:[["Double-blind trials","In a double-blind randomized controlled trial, volunteers are randomly given either the real treatment or a placebo, and nobody involved knows which until the end. If the real medicine works better than the placebo, scientists can be confident that it has a genuine effect."],["How it works","Brain scans show that when people expect pain relief from a placebo, areas involved in pain become less active. In some experiments, the effect was reduced by a drug that blocks endorphins, showing the body’s own painkillers play a role."],["Odd findings","Two placebo pills often work better than one, injections more than pills, and brand-name packaging more than plain. Placebo effects seem to have grown stronger in some American pain-drug trials in recent decades."],["Ethics","Giving a patient a placebo while telling them it is real medicine raises ethical concerns about honesty. Researchers have tested ‘open-label placebos’, where patients know they are getting a placebo, and found benefits for some conditions such as irritable bowel syndrome."]],
k:[["Placebo","A treatment with no active ingredient"],["Placebo effect","Feeling better because of expectation"],["Nocebo effect","Feeling side effects because of expectation"],["Used in","Clinical trials of new treatments"],["Works best for","Pain, nausea, tiredness, anxiety"]],
d:[["1785","The word placebo appears in a medical dictionary"],["1955","Henry Beecher’s paper The Powerful Placebo"],["1978","Study links placebo pain relief to endorphins"],["2010","Open-label placebo study for irritable bowel syndrome"]],
s2:[["The word","Placebo is Latin for ‘I shall please’."],["Fake surgery","Some trials compare real operations with ‘sham surgery’, in which patients receive small cuts but no real procedure. Several popular operations turned out to work no better than the sham."]],
g:[["Placebo","An inactive treatment"],["Nocebo","Harm caused by negative expectation"],["Double-blind","A study where neither patients nor researchers know who gets what"],["Endorphin","A natural painkiller made by the body"]],
f:["Placebos have no active ingredient.","Expectation can reduce pain.","New medicines are tested against placebos."],
fun:"In some studies, people felt more relief from a placebo pill they were told was expensive than from one they were told was cheap."});

ADDART({id:"hydration",t:"Water and the Body",c:"Health",i:"💧",w:"Body water",r:["kidney","nutri","extremeheat","digest","blood"],
p:[["Mostly water","About 50 to 60 percent of an adult’s body is water."],["Lost every day","We lose water by breathing, sweating and urinating."],["Thirst is a guide","Thirst and urine color help show if you need more."]],
l:"Water makes up about 50 to 60 percent of an adult’s body weight and even more of a baby’s. It is found in and around every cell, in blood and in fluids such as saliva and the fluid that cushions the brain and joints. Water carries nutrients and oxygen to cells, removes waste through urine, helps digest food, keeps joints moving smoothly and controls body temperature through sweating. We lose water constantly, through breathing out, sweating, urinating and in feces, so it must be replaced through drinks and food; fruits, vegetables and soups also contain a lot of water. How much water a person needs depends on their size, activity, the weather and their health. The body controls its water balance carefully: when it is low, the brain triggers thirst and the kidneys make less, darker urine. Losing too much water causes dehydration, with symptoms such as thirst, headache, tiredness and dizziness, and severe dehydration can be dangerous, especially for babies, older people and people with diarrhea or in very hot weather. Drinking far too much water too quickly can also be harmful, though this is rare.",
s:[["Where the water is","About two-thirds of the body’s water is inside cells. The rest is outside cells, in blood plasma and in the fluid between cells. The brain and heart are about 73 percent water, and even bones are about 31 percent water."],["Balance","Sensors in the brain detect when blood becomes too concentrated. The brain then releases a hormone called antidiuretic hormone, which tells the kidneys to hold on to water, and also creates the feeling of thirst."],["How much?","Health authorities give rough guides: for example, the European Food Safety Authority suggests total water from food and drinks of about 2 liters a day for women and 2.5 liters for men, but needs vary widely. Pale yellow urine is generally a good sign."],["Dehydration and diarrhea","Diarrhea, especially in young children, can quickly cause dangerous dehydration. Oral rehydration solution, a simple mix of water, salt and sugar, has saved millions of lives since the 1970s."]],
k:[["Share of adult body","About 50–60 percent"],["Inside cells","About two-thirds of body water"],["Daily water lost","Through breath, sweat, urine and feces"],["Control system","Thirst and the kidneys"],["Lifesaving treatment","Oral rehydration solution"]],
d:[["1968","Oral rehydration therapy tested successfully in Bangladesh and India"],["1971","Oral rehydration used widely during a refugee crisis in India"],["2010","EFSA publishes reference values for water intake"]],
s2:[["Babies","Newborn babies are about 75 percent water, which is one reason they can become dehydrated quickly."],["Not just drinks","About a fifth of the water most people take in comes from food rather than drinks."]],
g:[["Dehydration","Losing more water than you take in"],["Plasma","The liquid part of blood"],["Antidiuretic hormone","A hormone that makes the kidneys save water"],["Oral rehydration solution","A drink of water, salt and sugar that treats dehydration"]],
f:["Water makes up over half of an adult’s body.","Thirst and the kidneys control water balance.","Diarrhea can cause dehydration."],
fun:"You lose a little water every time you breathe out; you can see it as mist on a cold day."});

ADDART({id:"surrealism",t:"Surrealism",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🫠",w:"Surrealism",r:["modernart","renais","poetry","psychology","impress"],
p:[["Art from dreams","Surrealists explored dreams and the unconscious mind."],["Melting clocks","Salvador Dalí’s soft clocks are its most famous image."],["Founded in 1924","André Breton wrote the Surrealist Manifesto."]],
l:"Surrealism was an art and literary movement that began in Paris in the 1920s and sought to unlock the creative power of the unconscious mind, the hidden thoughts and desires that appear in dreams. It was founded by the French poet André Breton, who published the Surrealist Manifesto in 1924, inspired partly by the ideas of the psychologist Sigmund Freud and by the earlier anti-art movement Dada. Surrealist artists created strange, dreamlike scenes in which ordinary objects appear in impossible combinations or settings. Salvador Dalí painted melting pocket watches in The Persistence of Memory (1931); René Magritte painted a pipe with the words ‘This is not a pipe’; and Max Ernst, Joan Miró and Yves Tanguy invented new techniques and imaginary worlds. Surrealists also used methods such as automatic drawing and writing, letting the hand move without conscious control, and games such as the ‘exquisite corpse’. Surrealism spread to film, photography, fashion and literature around the world, and its influence can still be seen in art, advertising and cinema today.",
s:[["Famous works","Dalí’s The Persistence of Memory is in the Museum of Modern Art in New York. Magritte’s The Treachery of Images (1929) reminds viewers that a painting of a pipe is not a real pipe. Meret Oppenheim’s Object (1936) is a teacup, saucer and spoon covered in fur."],["Women surrealists","Leonora Carrington, Remedios Varo, Dorothea Tanning, Leonor Fini and Meret Oppenheim made major contributions. The Mexican painter Frida Kahlo was claimed by Breton as a surrealist, though she said she painted her own reality, not dreams."],["Games and methods","In ‘exquisite corpse’, several people each draw or write part of a figure or sentence without seeing what the others did, producing surprising results. Frottage, invented by Max Ernst, involves rubbing pencil over textured surfaces to suggest images."],["Spreading worldwide","Surrealist groups formed in many countries, from Belgium and Czechoslovakia to Japan, Egypt, Mexico and the Caribbean. The Martinican poet Aimé Césaire connected surrealism with the fight against colonialism."]],
k:[["Began","1924, Paris"],["Founder","André Breton"],["Key idea","Expressing the unconscious mind"],["Famous artists","Salvador Dalí, René Magritte, Max Ernst, Joan Miró, Leonora Carrington"],["Famous work","The Persistence of Memory (1931)"]],
d:[["1916","Dada movement begins in Zurich"],["1924","Surrealist Manifesto published"],["1929","Magritte’s The Treachery of Images"],["1931","Dalí’s The Persistence of Memory"],["1936","International Surrealist Exhibition in London"]],
s2:[["Film","The 1929 short film Un Chien Andalou, made by Luis Buñuel and Salvador Dalí, is a famous surrealist film full of shocking, dreamlike images."],["Surreal","Today, people call any bizarre, dreamlike situation ‘surreal’, a word that comes from the movement."]],
g:[["Surrealism","An art movement exploring dreams and the unconscious"],["Unconscious","Thoughts and feelings we are not aware of"],["Automatism","Creating without conscious control"],["Manifesto","A public statement of aims"]],
f:["Surrealism was founded by André Breton.","Dalí painted melting clocks.","Surrealists were inspired by dreams."],
fun:"Dalí said the idea for his melting clocks came from watching a Camembert cheese soften in the sun."});

ADDART({id:"cubism",t:"Cubism",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🧊",w:"Cubism",r:["picasso","modernart","renais","sculpture","impress","architecture"],
p:[["Many views at once","Cubist painters showed objects from several angles together."],["Picasso and Braque","Two artists invented it around 1907–1908."],["A revolution in art","It changed how artists represented the world."]],
l:"Cubism was an art movement, begun in Paris around 1907 by Pablo Picasso and Georges Braque, that broke away from the centuries-old tradition of painting objects from a single viewpoint. Instead, cubist artists broke subjects, such as people, guitars, bottles and landscapes, into geometric shapes and showed them from several angles at the same time, as if the viewer were walking around them. The name came from a critic who mocked one of Braque’s landscapes as being made of ‘little cubes’. Picasso’s painting Les Demoiselles d’Avignon (1907), with its angular figures influenced by Iberian sculpture and African masks, is often seen as a starting point. Cubism is usually divided into two phases: analytic cubism (about 1909 to 1912), with muted browns and grays and objects broken into many small facets, and synthetic cubism (from about 1912), with brighter colors, simpler shapes and collage, gluing real materials such as newspaper and wallpaper onto the canvas. Cubism influenced nearly every later art movement, as well as architecture, design and sculpture.",
s:[["Two friends","Picasso, from Spain, and Braque, from France, worked so closely between 1908 and 1914 that their paintings can be hard to tell apart. Braque later compared them to two mountaineers roped together. Their partnership ended when Braque went to fight in the First World War."],["Collage","In 1912 Picasso glued a piece of oilcloth printed with a chair-caning pattern onto Still Life with Chair Caning, often called the first modern collage. Braque soon invented papier collé, gluing pieces of paper into drawings."],["Other cubists","Juan Gris, Fernand Léger, Robert and Sonia Delaunay, Albert Gleizes and Jean Metzinger developed their own versions. Cubist ideas also spread to sculpture, with artists such as Alexander Archipenko, and to Czech architecture and furniture."],["Influence","Cubism opened the way for abstract art, futurism in Italy, constructivism in Russia and many styles of modern design. It showed that a painting did not have to copy appearance to represent reality."]],
k:[["Began","About 1907–1908, Paris"],["Founders","Pablo Picasso and Georges Braque"],["Key idea","Showing several viewpoints at once"],["Phases","Analytic and synthetic cubism"],["Key early work","Les Demoiselles d’Avignon (1907)"]],
d:[["1907","Picasso paints Les Demoiselles d’Avignon"],["1908","A critic mocks Braque’s ‘little cubes’"],["1912","First cubist collages"],["1914","Picasso and Braque’s partnership ends with war"]],
s2:[["Cézanne’s influence","The French painter Paul Cézanne, who said nature could be treated through the cylinder, sphere and cone, strongly influenced the cubists."],["Prague’s cubist houses","Prague has buildings in cubist style, with angular facades, such as the House of the Black Madonna, built in 1912."]],
g:[["Cubism","An art style showing objects as geometric shapes from several viewpoints"],["Collage","Art made by gluing materials onto a surface"],["Facet","One of many flat surfaces"],["Abstract art","Art that does not try to show recognizable things"]],
f:["Cubism was invented by Picasso and Braque.","Cubists showed several viewpoints at once.","Synthetic cubism used collage."],
fun:"The name cubism began as an insult from an art critic."});

ADDART({id:"popart",t:"Pop Art",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🥫",w:"Pop art",r:["modernart","comics","renais","print","photography"],
p:[["Art from everyday life","Pop artists used images from ads, comics and products."],["Soup cans and comic strips","Andy Warhol and Roy Lichtenstein made it famous."],["Bright and bold","It used flat colors and mass-production methods."]],
l:"Pop art is an art movement that emerged in Britain in the mid-1950s and in the United States in the late 1950s and 1960s, using images from popular and commercial culture, such as advertisements, comic books, packaging, celebrities and everyday products. It challenged the idea that art had to be serious or separate from ordinary life. In Britain, artists such as Richard Hamilton and Eduardo Paolozzi made collages from magazines and ads; Hamilton’s 1956 collage Just what is it that makes today’s homes so different, so appealing? is often called one of the first works of pop art. In the United States, Andy Warhol painted and silkscreen-printed Campbell’s soup cans, Coca-Cola bottles and portraits of stars such as Marilyn Monroe, repeating them as if mass-produced. Roy Lichtenstein copied comic-strip panels in huge size, complete with speech bubbles and the printing dots called Ben-Day dots. Claes Oldenburg made giant soft sculptures of hamburgers and lipsticks. Pop art’s bold colors and playful style remain hugely influential in design, fashion and advertising.",
s:[["Andy Warhol","Warhol worked as a commercial illustrator before becoming an artist. His 1962 exhibition of 32 Campbell’s Soup Cans paintings, one for each flavor, caused a sensation. His New York studio, called the Factory, produced prints, films and music. He predicted that in the future everyone would be ‘world-famous for 15 minutes’."],["Roy Lichtenstein","Lichtenstein’s paintings such as Whaam! (1963), showing a fighter plane firing a rocket, look like enlarged comic frames. He painted the dots by hand using stencils, imitating cheap printing."],["Around the world","Pop art had versions in many countries. In Japan, artists such as Tadanori Yokoo created psychedelic posters; in Brazil and Argentina, artists used pop imagery to comment on politics. Later artists such as Keith Haring and Takashi Murakami continued pop ideas."],["Critics and fans","Some critics thought pop art celebrated consumerism; others saw it as a clever comment on a world saturated with advertising and mass media. Either way, it blurred the line between ‘high’ art and popular culture."]],
k:[["Began","Mid-1950s (Britain), late 1950s–1960s (United States)"],["Subjects","Ads, comics, products, celebrities"],["Famous artists","Andy Warhol, Roy Lichtenstein, Richard Hamilton, Claes Oldenburg"],["Techniques","Silkscreen printing, Ben-Day dots, collage"],["Famous work","Campbell’s Soup Cans (1962)"]],
d:[["1956","Hamilton’s collage shown in London"],["1962","Warhol shows Campbell’s Soup Cans"],["1963","Lichtenstein paints Whaam!"],["1964","Warhol founds the Factory studio"]],
s2:[["Name","The term pop art was used by the British critic Lawrence Alloway in the 1950s, short for popular art."],["Record prices","Warhol’s Shot Sage Blue Marilyn sold at auction in 2022 for about 195 million US dollars, one of the highest prices ever paid for a 20th-century artwork."]],
g:[["Pop art","Art using images from popular culture"],["Silkscreen","A printing method pushing ink through a mesh"],["Ben-Day dots","Small colored dots used in cheap printing"],["Consumerism","A focus on buying goods"]],
f:["Pop art used images from ads and comics.","Andy Warhol painted soup cans.","Roy Lichtenstein copied comic strips."],
fun:"Warhol painted Campbell’s soup because, he said, he had eaten it for lunch nearly every day for 20 years."});

ADDART({id:"hollywood",t:"Hollywood and the Film Industry",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎬",w:"Cinema of the United States",r:["cinema","bollywood","anim","filmfestivals","streaming","documentary"],
p:[["A movie capital","Hollywood in Los Angeles became the center of American filmmaking."],["The studio system","Big studios once controlled actors, films and cinemas."],["A worldwide industry","Film industries thrive in India, Nigeria, China, Korea and beyond."]],
l:"Hollywood is a neighborhood of Los Angeles, California, whose name has become shorthand for the American film industry. Filmmakers moved there in the 1910s, attracted by sunny weather, varied landscapes and distance from patent lawsuits on the East Coast. In the studio era, from the 1920s to the 1950s, a handful of major studios, including Paramount, MGM, Warner Bros., 20th Century Fox and Columbia, controlled film production, distribution and many cinemas, with actors, writers and directors under long-term contracts. This ‘golden age’ produced classics in every genre. After a 1948 court ruling forced studios to sell their cinemas, and television drew audiences away, Hollywood changed, giving more power to independent producers and stars. From the 1970s, blockbusters such as Jaws and Star Wars created the modern summer hit. Today Hollywood films are seen worldwide, alongside major film industries in India, Nigeria, China, South Korea, Japan, France and elsewhere. Streaming services have transformed how films are made and watched.",
s:[["How films are made","A film moves through development (writing and financing), pre-production (casting, design and planning), production (filming), post-production (editing, music, sound and visual effects) and distribution (release in cinemas, on streaming services or both)."],["The Academy Awards","The Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences has presented the Academy Awards, or Oscars, since 1929. The 2020 Korean film Parasite was the first film not in English to win Best Picture."],["Film industries around the world","India produces more films each year than any other country, in many languages; Bollywood in Mumbai makes Hindi films. Nigeria’s Nollywood is one of the world’s largest by number of films. China has one of the largest cinema markets, and South Korean and Japanese films and animation have huge global followings."],["Changing times","In 2023 Hollywood writers and actors went on long strikes over pay in the streaming era and the use of artificial intelligence, winning new protections. Studios have increasingly released films on streaming services, sometimes at the same time as in cinemas."]],
k:[["Location","Los Angeles, California"],["Studio era","About 1920s–1950s"],["Oscars first presented","1929"],["Most films produced per year","India"],["Recent change","Streaming services"]],
d:[["1910","D. W. Griffith films in Hollywood"],["1923","The Hollywood sign is built (as ‘Hollywoodland’)"],["1927","The Jazz Singer, the first feature with synchronized speech"],["1948","Court ruling ends studio ownership of cinemas"],["1975","Jaws launches the summer blockbuster"],["2023","Writers’ and actors’ strikes"]],
s2:[["The sign","The Hollywood sign was built in 1923 to advertise a housing development called Hollywoodland. The last four letters were removed in 1949."],["Walk of Fame","The Hollywood Walk of Fame has more than 2,700 stars set in the sidewalk honoring people in film, television, music and radio."]],
g:[["Studio system","When big studios controlled all parts of filmmaking"],["Blockbuster","A very popular, high-earning film"],["Post-production","Editing and finishing a film after filming"],["Distribution","Getting films to audiences"]],
f:["Hollywood is in Los Angeles.","The Oscars began in 1929.","India produces the most films each year."],
fun:"The Hollywood sign originally read ‘Hollywoodland’."});

ADDART({id:"folkmusic",t:"Folk Music Around the World",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🪕",w:"Folk music",r:["musicals","worlddance","indianmusic","jazz","rockpop","orchestra"],
p:[["Music of the people","Folk music is passed down through communities."],["Learned by ear","Songs often spread by listening, not written notes."],["Every culture has it","From flamenco to fado, from griots to bluegrass."]],
l:"Folk music is the traditional music of a community or people, usually passed from person to person and generation to generation by ear rather than in writing. Folk songs often have no known composer, and they change as they are sung by different people, so many versions of the same song may exist. They tell stories of love, work, history, hardship, celebration and faith, and accompany everyday activities such as farming, sailing, weaving and dancing. Every culture in the world has its own folk traditions and instruments: the griots of West Africa sing histories accompanied by the kora harp; Portuguese fado expresses longing; Spanish flamenco combines song, guitar and dance; Irish and Scottish fiddle tunes accompany dances; Andean music features panpipes and the charango; Mongolian throat singing produces two notes at once; and American bluegrass and country grew from the music of immigrants and enslaved Africans. In the 20th century, folk music inspired revivals and protest songs, and its melodies and instruments continue to shape popular music worldwide.",
s:[["Collecting songs","From the late 1800s, collectors such as Cecil Sharp in England and the Appalachian mountains, Béla Bartók in Hungary and Romania, and Alan Lomax in the United States and many other countries recorded and wrote down thousands of folk songs, preserving traditions that might otherwise have been lost."],["Folk revival","In the 1950s and 1960s, a folk revival in the United States and Britain made singers such as Pete Seeger, Joan Baez and Bob Dylan famous. Folk songs became anthems of the civil rights and peace movements."],["Living heritage","UNESCO lists many folk traditions as Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity, including flamenco, fado, Mongolian khöömei throat singing, the Cuban rumba and the Korean song Arirang."],["Instruments","Folk instruments include the banjo, fiddle, accordion, bagpipes, sitar, balalaika, djembe drum, kora, charango, erhu and many kinds of flutes and drums, often made from local materials."]],
k:[["Passed on","By ear, from generation to generation"],["Composers","Often unknown"],["Examples","Fado, flamenco, griot songs, bluegrass, throat singing"],["Famous collectors","Cecil Sharp, Béla Bartók, Alan Lomax"],["Protected heritage","Many traditions listed by UNESCO"]],
d:[["1882","Francis Child begins publishing English and Scottish ballads"],["1906","Bartók begins collecting Hungarian folk songs"],["1930s","Alan Lomax records folk music across the United States"],["1960s","Folk revival and protest songs"],["2010","Flamenco added to UNESCO’s heritage list"]],
s2:[["Changing songs","The same ballad can appear in dozens of versions in different countries, with names, places and endings changed as it traveled."],["Work songs","Sea shanties helped sailors pull ropes in rhythm. In 2021 shanties became an online craze through short videos."]],
g:[["Folk music","Traditional music of a community"],["Oral tradition","Passing knowledge by word of mouth"],["Griot","A West African storyteller and musician"],["Revival","A renewal of interest in something old"]],
f:["Folk music is usually passed on by ear.","Griots are West African musicians and historians.","UNESCO protects some folk traditions."],
fun:"Mongolian throat singers can produce a low drone and a high whistling melody at the same time."});
